Model checking can answer whether a finite model reaches a bad state, even when the program generating that model has a much larger state space. Abstraction buys this reduction by retaining more possible transitions than the program actually has. The important distinction is between a witness in the model and a witness in the program. Dennis Dams's \emph{Abstract Interpretation and Partition Refinement for Model Checking} relates the choice of abstraction to the properties that survive it. David Arlen Schmidt's \emph{Data Flow Analysis Is Model Checking of Abstract Interpretations} explains the role of simulation in transferring results back from an abstract interpretation.

\section{The Safety Guarantee of a May Graph}

Take states $(\ell,s)$ consisting of a control location and a store. Partition stores at each location using chosen predicates and let $[q]$ denote the class containing state $q$. The ideal abstract graph contains an edge from $A$ to $B$ whenever some concrete transition goes from a member of $A$ to a member of $B$; a computable approximation may add edges but must not omit any of these. Mark as initial every class meeting the initial set and as potentially bad every class meeting the bad set. In particular, a mixed class containing good and bad states is not marked safe merely because one representative is good.

Every concrete prefix projects to a path in this graph: its first class is initial and each step contributes an abstract edge. Thus if there is no abstract path to any potentially bad class, there is no concrete bad prefix. This proof is particular to the direction of simulation and the safety query. An abstract path by itself does not establish a concrete existential property, and may edges can supply cycles with no concrete execution following them indefinitely. A liveness theorem needs preservation conditions for its particular temporal operators and for any fairness assumptions, not just a successful run of a graph algorithm.

\section{One Spurious Path and One Useful Predicate}

Imagine a controller that reads a Boolean request $r$ at entry. On its true branch it reaches a location $t$ and sets $p:=1$; on the false branch it reaches $f$ and sets $p:=0$. Both arms then join at $j$. The controller takes the grant edge only if $p=1$. The requirement is that grant is never reached with $r$ false. If the abstraction uses only control locations, the model checker can report the path through $f$, $j$, and grant; the incoming edge through $f$ is feasible and the grant edge from $j$ is feasible, but the two edges use different members of the same class at $j$.

Replay that path as a formula over successive concrete states. Write $p_j$ for the value at $j$ and keep the same symbol $r$ throughout because the request never changes. The relevant constraints, with the bad predicate written last, are
\[
  \neg r \;\land\; p_j=0 \;\land\; p_j=1 \;\land\; \neg r.
\]
The prefix establishes $p_j=0$, which separates it from the suffix's demand $p_j=1$. Splitting only $j$ on $p=1$ removes the path through $f$ to grant. But if the grant location is still one mixed class, the may graph can then report a path through $t$ to a potentially bad grant state. For a sufficient partition, track $r$ through the grant location, and distinguish both $r$ and $p=1$ at $j$. The reachable classes at $j$ then satisfy $(r,p)=(0,0)$ or $(1,1)$; only the second has a grant edge, and preservation of $r$ on that edge takes it to the good grant class. This is an inductive fact about the revised graph, not merely a rerun that happened to stop reporting one path.

\section{A Refinement Loop with Explicit Outcomes}

Counterexample-guided abstraction refinement alternates three operations: search the current abstract graph, check an alleged bad path against the concrete transition relation, and, if the path is infeasible, choose information that blocks the reason for infeasibility. The path check must include actual entry constraints and the actual bad predicate at the end. A satisfying valuation yields a concrete witness to replay; an unsatisfiable formula can yield a cut predicate from a conflicting set of constraints or an interpolant shared between prefix and suffix. A solver timeout is neither an infeasibility proof nor a violation. When precision is iteratively added, check that the new graph still covers all concrete steps, including steps at points not mentioned in the counterexample. The process need not terminate on an infinite-state model or with an insufficient predicate language. For finite Boolean obligations, Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-217} shows how to separate the solver's search from checking its answer.

The dossier's \emph{Counterexample-Guided Compiler Synthesis} uses a related loop but changes rewrite candidates in response to concrete disagreements. Here the program stays fixed and the verifier changes its model in response to spurious disagreements. In either loop, store the observation predicate with the artifact: changing what counts as bad can invalidate an earlier result without changing a single program instruction.
